# Contexta / Membit Integration with n8n

If you landed here thinking you could just copy the documentation from Membit’s website and have it work… stop. That documentation is **incomplete, and in some cases restricted** to specific developer accounts or beta programs. Most of the methods listed there simply do not work unless you’re running a very specific program. I wasted hours sifting through dead links, outdated guides, and broken examples just to figure out a usable path.  

Here’s the reality: the official MCP (Model Context Protocol) workflow is fancy, it’s shiny, but unless you’re in that beta/dev program, you can’t touch it. You *can*, however, integrate Membit with your products on a **less complex, fully functional scale** using their REST API and n8n.  

This is how I did it.
---

## Approach We’re Using

We are **not using MCP** in this workflow. Instead, we rely on **direct REST API calls** using HTTP Request nodes in n8n.

### REST API vs MCP

**REST API (Our Approach):**

- Direct HTTP calls to Membit endpoints (`https://api.membit.ai/v1/clusters/search`)  
- Simple request → response pattern  
- No AI agent required  
- Fully under your control  

**MCP (Model Context Protocol – Official, Not Used Here):**

- Protocol designed for AI agents (ChatGPT, Claude, etc.) to access external tools  
- Requires an MCP server running that the AI can connect to  
- AI decides when/how to call tools  
- Restricted access; mostly for beta/developer accounts  

We bypass MCP entirely, giving a simpler, more reliable solution.

---

## Authentication – Header Auth

This is where most workflows fail. Membit **requires header-based authentication**. If the API key is wrong or the header is misconfigured, your workflow will appear alive but return **no data**.  

**Rules for REST API Auth:**
use an header auth node and create credentials, add your membit api key and name.

1. Use an **HTTP Request node** in n8n  
2. Method: GET or POST depending on endpoint  
3. URL example: `https://api.membit.ai/v1/clusters/search?q=crypto&limit=10&format=json`  
4. **Headers:**  Key: X-Membit-Api-Key Value: <your_api_key_here>


5. Make sure your API key is valid — expired or miscopied keys will cause zero feedback  
6. Do not try to authenticate in the body or URL — Membit only accepts the `X-Membit-Api-Key` header  

---

## Example HTTP Request Node Configuration in n8n

- **Method:** GET  
- **URL:** `https://api.membit.ai/v1/clusters/search?q=crypto&limit=10&format=json`  
- **Headers:**  
  - `X-Membit-Api-Key` → `YOUR_KEY_HERE`  
- **Response Format:** JSON  
- **Authentication:** None (handled via header key)

This setup reliably returns data, unlike MCP examples which expect an AI agent to manage the request.  

---

## Why Header Auth is Important

If you do anything else — pass API key in query params, body, or as a different header — Membit will return a `401` or an empty result. The workflow will look “alive” but you’ll get nothing.  

Header auth ensures:

- Security: Your key isn’t exposed in URLs or logs  
- Consistency: Every request is authorized the same way  
- Compatibility: Works with n8n HTTP nodes and most other no-code tools  

Remember: **X-Membit-Api-Key is the only valid header name for auth**. No variations, no capitalization tweaks, no “Authorization: Bearer” nonsense.

---

## Summary

- You can integrate Membit directly using the REST API  
- Use HTTP Request nodes in n8n with **header-based API key authentication**  
- Keep your API key correct and secure  
- Avoid MCP unless you have beta/dev access — it’s not necessary for real-time data workflows  

This method gives you **full control**, **reliable responses**, and is fully reproducible.  

Once you set this up, you can:

- Query trending clusters  
- Build automated alerts  
- Pipe data into Telegram, Slack, or your own dashboards  
- Do all this without relying on MCP or any AI agent  

This is the approach I used to finally get Membit working end-to-end in n8n. It’s simple, fast, and actually works.



